America has more deaths from the  coronavirus now than any other nation in  the world more than 22,000 people have  lost their lives more than 12,000 died  in this country last week alone and the  pandemic is also causing many to go  hungry thousands have been forced to  wait for hours in long lines at food  banks all across the country feeding  America the nation's largest network of  food banks reported a 98 percent  increase in demand Janet shamlian is in  East Texas with the challenge of helping  people in rural areas where there are  high poverty rates and long travel  distances good morning it's a time of  unprecedented demand everywhere but the  need may be most critical here in rural  America communities where pantries are  closing because food is scarce and  because the volunteers who staff them  are worried about their own health this  is Donna for long and her husband  Leonard their story of how little food  they have it's hard to hear sometimes we  just don't have it so he'll go one day  without eating so I eat and I'll go day  eating so and you know vice versa that's  right  the couple trading off on who gets a  meal they rely on disability payments  food stamps and a pantry that closed in  March because it could no longer get  food where will you go now for food what  pantry will you utilize there isn't  there isn't one that we can go to Jasper  County Texas had six pantries a few  weeks ago three cans of corn three mixed  vegetables now only three including this  one here on  no sir three pantries to serve a county  of roughly 35,000 we're almost 20% live  in poverty and many just lost their jobs  it's a densely wooded region of almost a  thousand square miles some driving forty  miles to get here you've been running  this pantry more than 30 years have you  ever seen demand like this  no it's doubled everything we're doing  is double double double just about every  week it doubles from one time to the  other pantries in rural areas are  closing experts say because they tend to  be staffed by senior volunteers for  their safety  many of them are unable to come in work  at those pantries and therefore those  pantries are not operational those that  are getting food aren't reaching  everyone in it this is an hour from  Donna and Leonard's house they can't  afford the gas it's humiliating and you  know having to admit that yeah we're  starving and things are tough but we're  not the only ones stories like Donna  furloughs are heartbreaking to hear and  hard to believe and sadly hers is not an  isolated case Janet shamlian reporting  on the deepening economic pain in this  country tied of course to the virus
 

